<title>BRAZIL: Brazil coffee crop may reach 17.8 mln bags-Caccer.</title>
<headline>Brazil coffee crop may reach 17.8 mln bags-Caccer.</headline>
<dateline>RIO DE JANEIRO 1996-11-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's 1997/98 coffee crop should fall to 17.8 million bags, of which 13.6 million bags will be arabica, said an official estimate from the coffee growers' association in Cerrado, Minas Gerais (Caccer).</p>
<p>Earlier this month a Caccer official said that the harvest could drop to 17.3 million bags against 24.5 million bags harvested in 1996/97.</p>
<p>The reduction Caccer attributed to the off-year in Brazil's bi-annual growing cycle in which there is a larger harvest every other year.</p>
<p>Caccer's official break-down by state follows.</p>
<p> MINAS GERAIS - south of Minas	 4.9 million</p>
<p>		  - Zona da Mata	   1.7 million</p>
<p>		  - Cerrado		  1.5 million</p>
<p> PARANA					1.9 million</p>
<p> RONDONIA and OTHERS		     2.0 million</p>
<p> ESPIRITO SANTO			    3.5 million</p>
<p> SAO PAULO				   2.3 million</p>
<p> TOTAL					17.8 million</p>
<p>Caccer will make its next estimate in the first half of February next year, it said.</p>
<p>-- Caroline Bulloch, Rio de Janeiro newsroom, 5521 5074151</p>
</text>
